In this guide we provide instructions on reproducing results from our paper. For this guide we assume,
that you have already read and completed the setup instructions.

## MRAM Benchmark

The results of this benchmark are reported in Table 4 of our paper. The benchmark relies only
on code part of the client library and its examples. It can be run on UPMEM and on Memclave,
which one is selected using a CMake option. To build the Memclave version, run the following
chain of commands in the guest VM:
```bash
cmake -DENABLE_UPMEM_BENCHMARK=OFF -B build-mram-memclave .
cmake --build build-mram-memclave --target mram
```
This should yield an executable `build-mram-memclave/mram`. This executable outputs measurements
formatted as CSV on the command line. Capture the output like this:
```bash
./build-mram-memclave/mram > mram.csv
```

Building the UPMEM version is quite similar, you run almost the same commands, but on a normal
UPMEM machine and not the VM:
```bash
cmake -DENABLE_UPMEM_BENCHMARK=ON -b build-mram-upmem .
cmake --build build-mram-upmem --target mram
```
Execution works the same way. Simply capture the output into a file:
```bash
./build-mram-upmem/mram > mram-upmem.csv
```

## Crypto Benchmark

We present the performance of sealed EM transfer in table 3 of our paper. This measurement is based
on a benchmark of ChaCha20's performance on PIM. The benchmark consists of two components, a subkernel
and a guest side program.

The guest side program is part of the client library examples. It can be build just like all other
examples in the client library, by running the following commands inside the guest VM and `ime-client-library`
folder:
```bash
cmake -B build .
cmake --build build --target crypto-bench
```

The build process for the crypto benchmark is similarly straight-forward: Assuming you're currently cd'd
into the `ime` folder, running the following chain of commands will build the ChaCha20 benchmark subkernel:
```bash
docker run -it --rm -v ./:/ime memclave:latest bash
cd /ime
cmake -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=/usr/share/upmem/cmake/dpu.cmake -B build
cmake --build build --target chacha-bench
```
Now simply move the `chacha-bench` subkernel to the guest VM and execute the benchmark. Results are written
into a CSV file passed as a parameter to the executable.

## PrIM Benchmarks

We have ported 16 benchmarks of the PrIM project to Memclave. The subkernel parts of the ported benchmarks
are part of the `ime` folder, the guest parts are part of the `ime-client-library` folder. Both subkernels and
guest side programs are build via CMake. We provide a script to run all PrIM benchmarks at once for Memclave,
as doing this manually would take too much time. Refer to the section below for usage instructions.

### Example of Building a Single PrIM Benchmark

To build the guest part of the MLP benchmarks, run the following commands in the `ime-client-library` folder:
```bash
cmake -B build .
cmake --build build --target ime-mlp-example
```

The subkernel can be build using the `memclave` docker container as a build environment. Assuming you are
currently cd'd into the `ime` folder, running the following chain of commands will build the MLP subkernel:
```bash
docker run -it --rm -v ./:/ime memclave:latest bash
cd /ime
cmake -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=/usr/share/upmem/cmake/dpu.cmake -B build
cmake --build build --target mlp-example
```

The `mlp-example` subkernel will be located in `./build/mlp-example`. This can now be moved the the guest system.
You will have to rename the subkernel to `mlp` in this case, as paths for the PrIM benchmarks are hardcoded in
some cases. Afterwards, you should be able to run `ime-mlp-example`
from within the build folder, assuming you have moved the `mlp` subkernel to `../build/`.

### Automated Benchmark Execution

We'll start with building the subkernel parts of the PrIM benchmark suite. For this, assuming you are
currently cd'd into the `ime` folder, run the following chain of commands to create the docker build
environment and compile all PrIM subkernels:
```bash
docker run -it --rm -v ./:/ime memclave:latest bash
cd /ime
cmake -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=/usr/share/upmem/cmake/dpu.cmake -B build
cmake --build build --target prim-subkernels
```
After compilation, move the subkernels out of the docker container and on to the memclave environment.
A way to do this, is to first tar the artifacts and then copy out the archive. Assuming your docker
container has id `4fb4dcbfef03`, the following commands will work:
```bash
# start inside the container in /ime

cd build
tar cf prim-subkernels.tar *-example

# run in a seperate terminal
docker cp 4fb4dcbfef03:/ime/build/prim-subkernels.tar ~/Downloads/
```
Then move the subkernels from your Downloads folder, to your memclave environment.
Unpack the into the `ime-client-library` folder in your Memclave environment. If that
folder does not exist yet, copy it over as well.

To compile the client side of the PrIM benchmarks, run the following commands in the
`ime-client-library` folder in the Memclave environment:
```bash
cmake -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -B build .
cmake --build build --target prim-benchmarks
```

There is a slight mismatch between the subkernel names expected by the benchmarks, and
the actual subkernel names. Rename them using the following command:
```bash
for name in $(ls | grep -e '-example'); do mv $name ${name%-example}; done
```

No you should be able to run the PrIM suite for Memclave using the `run.sh` script:
```bash
./run.sh all
```
Once the script has run through, the benchmark results are stored in the file `./build/prim_results.csv`.

<!-- TODO: Must include BFS dataset somehow in the ime-client-library/data folder -->

### PrIM Baseline

We ship the baseline PrIM benchmarks as part of this artifact as well. To get the baseline numbers, you'll
have to move to `prim-benchmarks` folder to your UPMEM execution environment. There you can simply
run the `run_prim.py` script, which should generate the `prim_results.csv` file. This file is already present
in the artifact and will be replaced:
```bash
python3 run_prim.py
```
The script may first download some test data from zenodo, that was published alongside the PrIM project.
Afterwards, it should finish in a bit more than a minute.

### Generating The Plot

The large plot presented in figure 4 is generated based on the two `prim_results.csv` files generated by Memclave
and the baseline. Both files have to be joined together, before the final plot generation script can be executed.
Assuming you've named the files `memclave.csv` and `prim.csv` respectively, the following will join both datasets into
one:
```bash
python3 plotting/concat_results.py memclave.csv prim.csv > final.csv
```
From the `final.csv` file you will be able to generate figure 4:
```
python3 plotting/plot_speedup.py --csv final.csv
```

### Evaluation of BFS

We highlight the BFS workload in more detail in the paper. Assuming you've already completed the PrIM reproduction steps,
there are only a few steps required for validating our BFS numbers as well. On the memclave environment, cd'd into
the `ime-client-library` folder, you can simply run the script `run_bfs.py` to obtain the more detailed BFS numbers:
```bash
python3 run_bfs.py --mode memclave --cwd ./build --graph-prefix ../data
```
This should generate the file `bfs_results.csv`. Similarly, on your UPMEM environment, you can obtain baseline
numbers by running the same script with different parameters, if you're cd'd into the `prim-benchmarks` folder:
```bash
python3 run_bfs.py --mode prim --cwd BFS
```

This also yields a file named `bfs_results.csv`. Rename the files to `memclave.csv` and `prim.csv` respectively.
Move them to the same system and run
```bash
sed -i --quiet '/Memclave/p' memclave.csv
sed -i '/Memclave/d' prim.csv
cat prim.csv memclave.csv > bfs_results.csv
```
to concat the results into one file. Afterwards, using the script `plotting/plot_bfs.py` found in the artifact,
you can generate the plot presented in Figure 6.

### Evaluation of MLP

The results for MLP are obtained in a similar way to that of BFS. You run the `run_mlp.py` script however
```
python3 run_mlp.py --mode memclave --cwd build
```
to generate the file `mlp_results.csv`. The same thing can be done on the UPMEM environment side to obtain baseline
results. Inside your `prim-benchmarks` folder, simply run
```
python3 run_mlp.py --mode prim --cwd MLP
```
which will generate the file `mlp_results.csv`. Copy both of them over to the same system and name them
`memclave.csv` and `prim.csv` respectively. Concat them using the following commands:
```bash
sed -i '/Memclave/d' prim.csv
sed --quiet -i '/Memclave/p' memclave.csv
cat prim.csv memclave.csv > mlp_results.csv
```

This data set can now be used to generate the plot from figure 5 using the `plotting/plot_mlp.py` script:
```
python3 plot_mlp.py --csv mlp_results.csv
```

## Microbenchmarks

The measurements reported in Table 2 and 3 can be reproduced using the `sk-load-bench` tool. Just like
most Memclave programs, it consists of a subkernel and a guest side program. Important for this tool in particular
though is, that it uses a modified trusted loader, which reports performance stats throughout the decryption
and load process. Since the TL is embedded as a binary in the FSL, which is embedded in the ci-switch as a binary,
we'll need to recompile the ci-switch with the modified TL. First move to the `ci-switch` folder on your *development*
environment and clear all build files:
```
cd ci-switch
rm -rf build
```
Then, inside the ci-switch folder, modify the `ime/CMakeLists.txt` file. Find the definition for `IME_REPORT_STATS`
and set it to `1`. You can also use the command below to enable statistics
```
sed -i 's/IME_REPORT_STATS=0/IME_REPORT_STATS=1/' ./ime/CMakeLists.txt
```
or this one to disable them (return to a normal build):
```
sed -i 's/IME_REPORT_STATS=1/IME_REPORT_STATS=0/' ./ime/CMakeLists.txt
```

Rebuilding the `ci-switch` is done via the following two commands:
```bash
cmake -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug -B build
cmake --build build --target ci-switch
```

If an instance of the `ci-switch` is running, you can stop it without killing the VM. Simply enter Ctrl-C twice
in the controlling terminal. Then launch the new version.

Once deployed, you can simply build the `sk-load-bench` subkernel in the `ime` folder, just like any other subkernel,
by first configuring `cmake` and then building the related target:
```bash
cmake -DMCAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=/usr/share/upmem/cmake/dpu.cmake -B build
cmake --build build --target sk-load-bench
```

The same goes for the guest side program also called `sk-load-bench`. Go into the `ime-client-library` folder that
should be present in your Memclave environment. If not, move it into the Memclave environment. Then run the following
two commands:
```bash
cmake -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -B build
cmake --build build --target sk-load-bench
```

Once both sides are compiled, move the subkernel into the `ime-client-library` folder of the Memclave environment
and cd into the `build` directory. Then you should be able to execute the `sk-load-bench` program. This should execute
for about 30s.

The tool will output load and unload timings per-DPU of one rank, and the duration the key exchange and DPU ready line took.
The DPU measurements are reported in DPU clock cycles. They accumulate, so to get the actual decrypt time, one
has to subtract the auth time first. Our DPUs are clocked at 350 Mhz. Therefore, each of the measurements
can be divided by 350000000 to obtain a measurement in seconds.

*/